# Reference verification, batch 3: s013-s019 (Nov 2026)
upd("n289", append="[Checked: Alexander of Alessandria’s *Sentences* commentary is unedited and no text of d. 35 q. 2 was found online. The attribution of the *quinto* proof to him could not be checked. Still open.]")
upd("n311", append="[Checked: Henry, *Quodl.* IX q. 15 (Badius 1518 via SCTA) treats the soul’s hidden self-knowledge (*notitia abdita*, after Aug. *De Trin.* X and XIV). The Henry half is verified. Ware’s *In I Sent.* is unedited, so the Ware half is still open.]", status="verified")
upd("n313", append="[Checked: Henry, *Quodl.* VI q. 1 (Badius 1518 via SCTA), ‘Utrum sint tres in divinis personae, nec plures nec pauciores’, argues from the two notional emanations by intellect and will and the uniqueness of the *innascibilis* (Hilary, *De synodis*). It fits the argument Scotus reports. Verified.]", status="verified")
upd("n314", append="[Checked in Badius 1520 and 1518 via SCTA: Henry, *Summa* a. 54 (‘de modo emanandi vnam personam ab alia’, 10 qq.; q. 2 asks whether the non-emanating person is only one) and *Quodl.* VI q. 1 both match the matching of essential and notional acts. Verified. The Vatican apparatus itself could not be consulted.]", status="verified")
upd("n318", append="[Checked: Scotus, *Quodl.* q. 1 (Vivès XXV, archive.org operaomnia25duns): ‘Ista distinctio de actu productivo et operativo patebit post q. 13. Actus enim operativus non habet terminum productum, sed est ultimus terminus … Actus autem productivus semper est alicuius…’. So *in primo quodlibeto* is *Quodl.* q. 1, and Scotus there points ahead to q. 13. Verified.]", status="verified")
upd("n325", append="[Checked: this is an internal cross-reference within Gorris’s own book, and there is no external source to check. It stays open until the page count can be checked against A.]")
upd("n327", text="Answering the *contra* of Deut 32:39 (‘videte quod ego sim solus’). The vocabulary of *solitudo* comes from Peter Lombard, *Sent.* I d. 23 c. 5 (‘Quod in Trinitate non est diversitas vel singularitas vel solitudo’) and d. 24 c. 1 (‘cum dicimus plures esse personas, singularitatem atque solitudinem excludimus’, with Hilary on *faciamus*), in the Quaracchi text via SCTA. The earlier ‘d. 24 c. 3’ was wrong. Scotus’s reply in *Ord.* I d. 2 p. 2 could not be checked against the Vatican paragraph numbers.", status="verified")
upd("n334", append="[Checked: Henry, *Summa* a. 22 (Badius 1520 via SCTA), q. 2: God is known naturally ‘inquantum cognoscitur ens simpliciter vel bonum simpliciter’; q. 3: known ‘aut indistincte & in vniuersali … [aut] attributis’. This is exactly the view Scotus discusses, so Gorris’s *a. 22 qq. 2–3* is right. Verified.]", status="verified")
upd("n345", text="Scotus’s reply to the second principal argument of the first question (Aristotle, *Metaph.* II 1, 993b9–11: our intellect is to the most evident things as the owl’s eye to sunlight). After setting aside Averroes’ reading (*de difficili*), Scotus says ‘oculus noctuae non habet cognitionem nisi intuitivam et naturalem … sic intellectui nostro est impossibile naturaliter et intuitive cognoscere Deum’ (*Ord.* I d. 3 p. 1 qq. 1–2, Wadding/Vivès text via SCTA). This is the only reply to a principal argument there that turns on intuitive knowledge. The earlier note’s ‘Jn 1:18’ was not found in d. 3. The identification is corrected, and Vatican paragraph numbers were not checked.", status="verified")
upd("n351", append="[Checked in Badius 1518 via SCTA: *Quodl.* XV q. 9 (whether the angel’s self-intellection is more essential to it than its intellection of God) answers ‘tam secundum rationem causae formalis quam … finalis deus est principale obiectum cuiuslibet intellectus’, which is the opinion Scotus attacks. *Quodl.* XV q. 4 is on food converted into Christ’s body and has no bearing. So A’s *15 q. 9* is right and B’s *xv q. iiij* wrong. XIII q. 9 is on the first object of the will (*bonum sub ratione convenientis*); it is a parallel only. Verified for XV q. 9.]", status="verified")
